{
  "name": "How to Prevent Moisture Damage to Bathroom Molding",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Moisture is the archenemy of bathroom molding, and if you don't address it, you'll be dealing with peeling and warping in no time. The first step in prevention is choosing the right material. While wood looks great, PVC moldings are often a better bet for bathrooms because they're inherently waterproof and can even be used around tubs and showers. They're designed to stand up to the humidity and splashes that are just a part of bathroom life. Proper installation is also key. Make sure the surface is squeaky clean and dry before you even think about applying adhesive. Don't skimp on a good quality waterproof adhesive, and ensure you apply enough pressure to create a strong bond. One of the most critical steps, often overlooked, is sealing all the edges with waterproof caulk. This creates a barrier against water seeping behind the molding and causing damage. Remember, even waterproof materials need their edges sealed for full protection. Lastly, good ventilation in your bathroom is a huge help. Running the fan during and after showers can significantly reduce overall humidity, giving your molding a much better chance at a long life. If you're tackling a complex layout or are unsure about proper waterproofing techniques, HH Remodeling LLC has the expertise to ensure your bathroom molding is installed to last.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
